# Gurbalib commands

## Command structure

All wizard/admin commands inherit `M_COMMAND`:

```lpc
inherit M_COMMAND;

string *usage(void) {
   string *lines;

   lines = ({ "Usage: mycmd [-h] ARG" });
   lines += ({ " " });
   lines += ({ "Description of what this command does." });
   lines += ({ " " });
   lines += ({ "Options:" });
   lines += ({ "\t-h\tHelp, this usage message." });
   lines += get_alsos();
   return lines;
}

void setup_alsos() {
   add_also("wiz", "update");
   add_also("wiz", "clone");
}

static void main(string arg) {
   /* command implementation */
}
```

## Required pieces

- `usage()` — returns array of help lines; always include `-h` option
- `setup_alsos()` — cross-references to related commands via `add_also(type, cmd)`
- `main(string arg)` — entry point (called by `M_COMMAND` framework as `static void main`)
- Also types: `"player"`, `"wiz"`, `"admin"`

## Path handling

Use `this_player()->query_env("cwd")` and `normalize_path()` for relative file paths. Append `.c` when missing:

```lpc
if ((str[strlen(str) - 2] == '.') && (str[strlen(str) - 1] == 'c')) {
   /* already has .c */
} else {
   str = str + ".c";
}
path = normalize_path(str, this_player()->query_env("cwd"));
```

## After adding a new command

1. Place the file in the correct `cmds/` subdirectory (`wiz/`, `admin/`, or player cmds)
2. In-game: **`rehash`** (admin) to register the command
3. Test with the command name and `-h` for usage

## Player commands

Player commands follow the same `M_COMMAND` pattern but live under `lib/cmds/` or `lib/sys/cmds/` depending on scope. Check neighboring commands in the same directory for registration patterns.

## Do not

- Bypass `M_COMMAND` with ad-hoc `add_action()` unless extending an object's local commands
- Use `eval` patterns in production command code
- Forget to handle the empty-arg and `-h` cases in `main()`
